---
import { Container } from '@components/react/Container';
import { Heading } from '@components/react/Heading';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, OWNER, SITE_TITLE } from '@/consts';
import { ANALYTICS, CDN, HOSTING, STORED_SETTINGS, THIRD_PARTY_HOSTS } from '@/data/privacy';
import Layout from '@/layouts/Page.astro';
---

<Layout
  title={`Privacy Policy | ${SITE_TITLE}`}
  description="What data this site processes, who receives it, and the rights you have over it."
>
  <Container as="main">
    <div class="px-4">
      <Heading noMargin className="mb-8">Privacy Policy</Heading>
      <div class="prose lg:prose-lg">
        <p>
          This site sets no cookies, runs no third-party advertising and no cross-site tracking. It
          shows one banner for a project of my own, which you can dismiss. It keeps a few of your
          settings in your browser and counts page views without identifying you. The detail is
          below.
        </p>

        <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
        <p>
          {OWNER}, contact <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. The full
          disclosure is on the <a href="/imprint">imprint</a> page.
        </p>

        <h2>Hosting and server logs</h2>
        <p>
          The site is hosted by {HOSTING.name}, {HOSTING.address}. Every time your browser requests
          a page, the technical data needed to deliver it — your IP address, the requested URL, the
          time, and your browser's user agent — is processed and kept briefly to run and secure the
          service. The legal basis is my legitimate interest in operating a website that works and
          stays available (Art. 6 (1) (f) GDPR). {HOSTING.dpa}
          {HOSTING.transfer}
        </p>
        {
          CDN && (
            <p>
              The site is served through the content delivery network of {CDN.name}, {CDN.address},
              which sits in front of the hosting and delivers pages from a location near you.
              Because it is the first server your browser talks to, {CDN.shortName} processes your
              IP address, the requested URL, the time, your browser's user agent and connection
              metadata in order to route the request, cache the response and block attacks. The
              legal basis is my legitimate interest in a fast and attack-resistant website (Art. 6
              (1) (f) GDPR). {CDN.dpa} {CDN.transfer} Its own privacy policy is at{' '}
              <a href={CDN.privacyPolicy}>{CDN.privacyPolicy}</a>.
            </p>
          )
        }

        {
          ANALYTICS && (
            <>
              <h2>Visitor statistics</h2>
              <p>
                I use {ANALYTICS.name} to see how many people read an article and where they came
                from. {ANALYTICS.name} documents that {ANALYTICS.method}. The legal basis is my
                legitimate interest in knowing which articles are worth writing more of (Art. 6 (1)
                (f) GDPR).
              </p>
              <p>Vercel records, for each page view:</p>
              <ul>
                {ANALYTICS.collects.map((item) => (
                  <li>{item}</li>
                ))}
              </ul>
              <p>
                I see these only as aggregate counts in a dashboard, never as one reader's history.
                The statistics work without storing anything in, or reading anything from, your
                browser, so they do not trigger the consent requirement in § 165 Abs 3 TKG 2021 and
                no consent banner is shown for them.
              </p>
            </>
          )
        }

        <h2>Settings stored on your device</h2>
        <p>These preferences live in your browser's local storage and are never sent anywhere:</p>
        <ul>
          {
            STORED_SETTINGS.map((setting) => (
              <li>
                <code>{setting.key}</code> — {setting.purpose}
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
        <p>
          These entries have no expiry date and stay in your browser until you change the setting
          again or clear them through your browser's site data settings. They contain nothing but
          the value shown above and are never transmitted to me or to anyone else, so nothing here
          recognises you across visits.
        </p>
        <p>
          Storing them, and reading them back when a page loads so the site renders the way you
          chose, is storage on and access to your terminal equipment within the meaning of § 165 Abs
          3 TKG 2021. No consent is required for it, because each entry exists solely to deliver the
          site the way you explicitly asked for it — that is the exemption for what is strictly
          necessary to provide a service the user expressly requested. Nothing here measures you.
        </p>

        <h2>Content loaded from other providers</h2>
        <p>
          A few things on this site are fetched from servers I do not run. When that happens, your
          IP address necessarily reaches that provider, because it is what delivers the content to
          you:
        </p>
        <ul>
          {
            THIRD_PARTY_HOSTS.map((provider) => (
              <li>
                <strong>{provider.name}</strong> — {provider.what}, loaded from{' '}
                {provider.hosts.map((host, index) => (
                  <>
                    {index > 0 && ' and '}
                    <code>{host}</code>
                  </>
                ))}
                .
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
        <p>
          The legal basis is my legitimate interest in illustrating articles with runnable examples
          (Art. 6 (1) (f) GDPR). CodeSandbox B.V. is established in the Netherlands, so this is not
          a transfer to a third country; CodeSandbox states in its own privacy policy that it may
          pass data to parties outside the EEA and that it does so only where there is an
          appropriate level of protection.
        </p>
        <p>
          Fonts, images and the donation button are all served from this site's own domain, so no
          other provider learns anything about you through them. Links to GitHub, Bluesky or
          LinkedIn are plain links: nothing is loaded from them until you click.
        </p>

        <h2>How long data is kept</h2>
        <p>
          {HOSTING.shortName} keeps its server logs {HOSTING.logRetention}.{' '}
          {
            CDN && (
              <>
                {CDN.shortName} keeps its logs {CDN.logRetention}.{' '}
              </>
            )
          }
          {ANALYTICS && <>Analytics data is {ANALYTICS.retention}.</>}
        </p>

        <h2>Your rights</h2>
        <p>
          I hold no personal data about you myself. Everything described here is processed by my
          providers in order to deliver the site, and a message to me is the fastest way to have me
          take it up with them. You have the right to access your data, to have it corrected or
          erased, to have its processing restricted, and to object to processing based on legitimate
          interest. Data portability under Art. 20 GDPR does not apply here, because nothing on this
          site is processed on the basis of consent or a contract. Write to <a
            href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a
          > and I will handle it. If you think I am getting this wrong, you can complain to the Austrian
          data protection authority, the Datenschutzbehörde (<a href="https://www.dsb.gv.at"
            >dsb.gv.at</a
          >), Barichgasse 40-42, 1030 Vienna.
        </p>
        <p>
          I do not use your data for automated decision-making or profiling within the meaning of
          Art. 22 (1) and (4) GDPR.
        </p>

        <h2>Your right to object</h2>
        <p>
          Where I process your data on the basis of my legitimate interests (Art. 6 (1) (f) GDPR) —
          the hosting, the delivery network, the visitor statistics and the embedded content
          described above — you have the right to object at any time, on grounds relating to your
          particular situation, under Art. 21 (1) GDPR. If you object, I will stop that processing
          unless I can demonstrate compelling legitimate grounds which override your interests,
          rights and freedoms. A message to the address above is enough; no form and no reason are
          required.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Container>
</Layout>
